\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Quaternion spinor gauge fields}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Take #{G=Q_8=\{\pm1,\pm i,\pm j,\pm k\}} in the unit
quaternions and its defining complex spinor representation
#{\sigma:Q_8\to SU(2)}. Its character is
#{\chi_\sigma(h)=2\operatorname{Re}h}. The 4 linear characters
factor through #{Q_8/\{\pm1\}\cong C_2\times C_2}.
The spinor has character norm #{(2^2+(-2)^2)/8=1}, so is
irreducible. These 5 inequivalent irreducibles are complete
because #{4\cdot1^2+2^2=8}. This is the quaternion representation
already used in the [group-algebra examples](fgap-001B).}

\p{For the [spinor Wilson weight](fdip-006V), put #{q=e^{-\beta}}.
Then
##{f_\beta(1)=1,\qquad f_\beta(-1)=q^2,\qquad
 f_\beta(\pm i)=f_\beta(\pm j)=f_\beta(\pm k)=q.}
Its irreducible character coefficients are
##{\begin{aligned}
a&=\frac{1+q^2+6q}{8} &&\text{(trivial)},\\
b&=\frac{(1-q)^2}{8} &&\text{(each other linear character)},\\
c&=\frac{1-q^2}{4} &&\text{(spinor)}.
\end{aligned}}
For a nontrivial linear character, the sum over the 6 noncentral
units is #{-2}; for the spinor it is zero, while the central values
are #{2,-2}. Substitution into the character inner product proves
all three formulas. The [surface theorem](fdip-006W) gives
##{Z_\Sigma(f_\beta)=a^P+3b^P+
 2^{2-2g}\left(\frac c2\right)^P.}
At #{\beta=0}, only #{a=1} remains. At the identity,
#{a+3b+2c=1}, checking the coefficient normalization.}

\p{A one-face torus has one vertex and two loop edges #{x,y}.
The square below identifies equally labelled sides in their arrow
directions and all 4 corners. A positive boundary traversal is
#{x,y,x^{-1},y^{-1}}. Writing #{A=U_x} and #{B=U_y}, its
holonomy is #{H=B^{-1}A^{-1}BA}.}

\tikzfig{
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.05,>=stealth]
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (2.6,0);
\coordinate (C) at (2.6,1.8);
\coordinate (D) at (0,1.8);
\draw[->] (A) -- node[below] {$x$} (B);
\draw[->] (B) -- node[right] {$y$} (C);
\draw[->] (D) -- node[above] {$x$} (C);
\draw[->] (A) -- node[left] {$y$} (D);
\node at (1.3,0.9) {$p$};
\foreach \v in {A,B,C,D} {\fill (\v) circle (1.8pt);}
\end{tikzpicture}
}

\p{The normalized Wilson loop is #{W_\sigma=\operatorname{Re}H}.
Of the #{64} ordered pairs #{(A,B)}, exactly #{40} commute:
the two central elements have centralizer size 8 and the 6
others size 4. Their commutator is #{1}; the other #{24}
commutators are #{-1}. Hence the direct finite sum gives
##{Z_{\mathbb T^2}(f_\beta)=\frac{5+3q^2}{8},\qquad
 \langle W_\sigma\rangle=\frac{5-3q^2}{5+3q^2}.}
The partition formula also follows from #{a+3b+c/2}.
This single plaquette is the whole torus, not a disk bounded by a
simple contractible loop of the earlier square-torus theorem.
Its boundary word can have a nontrivial holonomy because the
connection is not constrained to be flat.}
